WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.269
The euro in dollars, every trading day for twenty-seven years.

00:03.366 --> 00:07.041
Zoom in to seven years. Then to two. Then to half a year. The

00:07.141 --> 00:11.559
line never turns smooth: at every scale it looks the same. Which of these

00:11.659 --> 00:15.335
pictures is a quarter of a century, and which is half a year?

00:17.466 --> 00:20.409
Lines like this are random walks, and this lecture is

00:20.509 --> 00:23.165
about what they look like when we step far back.

00:24.233 --> 00:27.809
Take a fair coin: up one step for heads, down one for tails.

00:27.909 --> 00:31.792
Ten tosses make a staircase. Now squeeze time so that the T steps

00:31.892 --> 00:35.836
fit into the unit interval, and shrink the height by sigma root T.

00:35.933 --> 00:39.030
A hundred tosses, a thousand, ten thousand. The

00:39.130 --> 00:41.955
steps become invisible, and what is left is

00:42.055 --> 00:45.629
a continuous, endlessly jagged curve: Brownian motion.

00:46.000 --> 00:49.165
Many such paths, and their end points pile up into

00:49.265 --> 00:52.757
the bell curve. That bell is the ordinary central limit

00:52.857 --> 00:57.197
theorem. The functional version says more: the whole path converges,

00:57.297 --> 01:00.528
to the same Brownian motion, whatever coin we toss.

01:01.600 --> 01:04.588
The opening zoom hid a trick. Here is a random walk.

01:04.688 --> 01:08.448
Zoom honestly into its first quarter, by four in both directions.

01:08.533 --> 01:12.021
The path no longer fits the window. It is too wild. To make it

01:12.121 --> 01:15.956
look like the original, we must squeeze its height, but only by two.

01:18.533 --> 01:22.837
Time by four, height by two. Variances add up, so the spread of

01:22.937 --> 01:27.101
a random walk grows like the square root of time. That is why

01:27.201 --> 01:31.994
we divide by root T, and why Brownian motion is self-similar: speed up

01:32.094 --> 01:36.118
time by c, and it looks the same, only root c times larger.

01:37.200 --> 01:42.238
Three estimators, three speeds. Each car's distance is how much sharper

01:42.338 --> 01:46.146
its estimate has become, on a log scale, as the sample

01:46.246 --> 01:50.706
grows. The sample mean improves like root T. The coefficient of

01:50.806 --> 01:54.904
a unit root like T: superconsistent. The slope of a linear

01:55.004 --> 01:57.292
trend like T to the three halves.

01:57.366 --> 02:02.069
The reason is how fast each regressor piles up information, its sum of

02:02.169 --> 02:07.077
squares. A constant adds one each period. A random walk wanders away like

02:07.177 --> 02:11.331
root t, so its squares grow like T squared. A trend grows like

02:11.431 --> 02:14.899
T cubed. The rate is the square root of that growth.

02:15.966 --> 02:19.420
So: squeeze time by T and height by root T, and every

02:19.520 --> 02:23.979
random walk becomes the same Brownian motion. It is the lens through

02:24.079 --> 02:27.935
which we will read the unit root tests of the next lecture.
